{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update26.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update26.json → valid=true). New A1/A2 finding on leadership verification, recorded as conditional. The API says VerifyLeader 'is used to ensure this peer is still the leader' and may guard against stale reads (api.go:880-882). Its threshold is a majority of the voters (raft.go:1084-1094), but the pending verification is registered with *every* replication state (raft.go:963-987), replication state exists for every configured peer except the local server regardless of suffrage (raft.go:586-592), votes are counted as booleans with no sender information (future.go:274-293), and they are submitted by the replication heartbeat whenever any peer answers an AppendEntries (replication.go:98-111). Non-voters' acknowledgements therefore count toward the voter-majority threshold — e.g. in a 3-voter + 2-non-voter cluster, the leader plus two non-voters reaches the threshold of two. The same file contains the contrasting check: the leadership lease counts only voter-suffrage servers (raft.go:1048-1056), so the two mechanisms answer 'can I still reach a quorum' from different evidence and can disagree about the same cluster. What keeps the consequence conditional rather than a demonstrated stale read is the interaction between them: the lease ends the leader state within the lease timeout regardless of non-voter traffic, so the window in which a verification can succeed without voter contact is bounded by that timeout, and the default lease is short relative to the voters' election timeout; where it matters is a deployment that sets the lease long (validation permits up to the heartbeat timeout) while a quorum of voters is unreachable, since within that window the voters may already have elected a new leader. Counterevidence kept explicit — the lease bounds the window, all-voter clusters are unaffected, and a caller combining VerifyLeader with the documented lease assumption carries the same exposure any lease-based read does — and the discriminator is stated: isolate a leader from all voters while keeping non-voters connected and observe a verification succeeding with zero voter acknowledgements.\"}"}}
